# Working with awf

awf owns this project's generated skills, agents, docs, and `AGENTS.md`. Edit the committed
`.awf/` sources and convention parts, never a generated output. Run `./awf render` after a
source change and keep the regenerated outputs and `.awf/awf.lock` with that change.

This is the daily guide. [Workflow](workflow.md) owns effort lifecycle and model-selection
policy; [Debugging](debugging.md) owns recovery and failure diagnosis; and the
[configuration reference](config-reference.md) owns configuration and override semantics.
<!-- awf:end -->

{{if eq .profile "core"}}## Daily commands

- `awf init`: scaffold `.awf/` and render the selected workflow into the current project.
- `./awf render`: render the selected workflow from `.awf/` and update `.awf/awf.lock`.
- `./awf check`: report generated-file drift and repository checks.
- `./awf upgrade`: migrate configuration and re-render after an awf upgrade.

Use `.awf/` sources and convention parts to customize the generated workflow, then render,
check, and commit the managed outputs and lock together. Project documentation, source, tests,
and repository history remain the authority for Core work.
{{else}}<!-- awf:section commands -->
## Daily commands

- `awf init`: scaffold `.awf/` and render the selected governance footprint into the current project.
- `./awf render`: re-render managed artifacts from `.awf/` and update `.awf/awf.lock`.
- `./awf check`: confirm the rendered tree and repository checks are clean.
- `./awf upgrade`: apply pending configuration migrations and re-render after upgrading awf.
- `./awf config [<key-or-var>]`: inspect effective configuration.

Use the repository `./awf` wrapper for local commands. For workflow and effort commands, see
[Workflow](workflow.md); for a command refusal, recovery, or upgrade residue, see
[Debugging](debugging.md).
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section config-and-overrides -->
## Config and overrides

Configuration lives in `.awf/config.yaml`, artifact sidecars, and convention parts. The
[configuration reference](config-reference.md) is the single home for every key and for
placeholder semantics. A convention part replaces one declared section; use its
`awf:edit` pointer to find the source, then render and check.

Generated files are awf-owned. An `awf:edit` pointer names a convention-part source; a topic
page names its metadata and claim part. Local documents are the exception: edit only their body
between `awf:edit-in-place` and `awf:end`. See the [documentation standard](doc-standard.md) for
authorship rules.
<!-- awf:end -->

